--TEST--
`Core\Http\Response::jsonAs<T>()` refuses every reply that is not the document it was asked for — one that carried no body, one whose octets are not text, and one nested past the call's own `maxDepth` — while `array<T>` reads a JSON array as one `T` per element — `rule:errors/ambiguous-input-refused`, `rule:core-classes/derive-field-list`
--FILE--
<?nvs
#[Core\Json\Derive]
class Point {
    public int $x;

    public function constructor(int $x) {
        $this->x = $x;
    }
}

// A reply that carried no body is not the document `null`: nothing arrived,
// and a program that asked for a `Point` is told so rather than handed an
// instance built out of nothing.
Core\Test::answerHttp("https://api.example.com/empty", 204, {body: ""});
var $empty = Core\Http\Client::get("https://api.example.com/empty");
try {
    $empty->jsonAs<Point>();
} catch (ParseError $noBody) {
    echo "no body: refused\n";
}

// The octets `text` refuses for not being UTF-8 reach the same refusal here by
// a shorter route: JSON is text, so bytes that are not text spell no document
// at all, and that is this member's `ParseError` rather than `text`'s
// `RuntimeError`.
Core\Test::answerHttp("https://api.example.com/blob", 200, {body: Core\Encoding::fromHex("ff00")});
var $blob = Core\Http\Client::get("https://api.example.com/blob");
try {
    $blob->jsonAs<Point>();
} catch (ParseError $notText) {
    echo "not text: refused\n";
}

// `array<T>` is the same decode run once per element, and the `maxDepth` that
// refuses it is the call's own bound — nothing the reply declared can raise or
// lower it.
Core\Test::answerHttp("https://api.example.com/points", 200, {body: "[{\"x\":1},{\"x\":2}]"});
var $points = Core\Http\Client::get("https://api.example.com/points");
echo Core\Arr::count($points->jsonAs<array<Point>>()), "\n";
try {
    $points->jsonAs<array<Point>>({maxDepth: 2});
} catch (ParseError $tooDeep) {
    echo "too deep: refused\n";
}
--EXPECT--
no body: refused
not text: refused
2
too deep: refused
